deep.reconstruction:
Setting. X is a smooth (moduli) stack, here M̄_{g,ν}, and D is its boundary divisor with normal crossings. For each d there are:
- D_d, the closed locus where at least d branches of D meet;
- D^!_d, the étale cover that orders or labels those branches;
- T_d, a rank-d local system of free Z-modules on D_d with fibre Z^{I_d}. It is the lattice of local branches, twisted by the monodromy that permutes them.
- G_d = T_d ⊗_Z G_m, a twisted torus of rank d over D_d;
- P_d, a G_d-torsor over D_d. It is almost certainly the torsor of 'frames of the normal bundle', meaning the fibre product of the punctured normal line bundles N_i^× of the d branches. This is the algebraic model of the boundary of a tubular neighbourhood of the stratum.

The incidence correspondence. For d ≥ d', D_{d,d'} parametrises a point of D_d together with a choice of d' of its d branches. On the labelled cover, D^!_{d,d'} is the bundle of d'-element subsets of I_d over D^!_d (diagram (195), previous page). On D_{d,d'} one has two lattices:
- T(d,d')_d, the pullback of T_d;
- T(d,d')_{d'}, the pullback of T_{d'};
and a tautological surjection (194) between them. On the labelled cover it is the coordinate projection Z^{I_d} → Z^{J} for the chosen J.

This page.
- (197): tensoring (194) with G_m gives a morphism of tori G(d,d')_d → G(d,d')_{d'}, defined as the pullbacks G_d ×_{D_d} D_{d,d'} and G_{d'} ×_{D_{d'}} D_{d,d'}. It is a surjection whose kernel is a torus, hence a smooth epimorphism.
- (198): the pulled-back d'-torsor is the pushout (contracted product) of the pulled-back d-torsor along this torus morphism: P(d,d')_{d'} ≅ P(d,d')_d ∧^{G(d,d')_d} G(d,d')_{d'}. Concretely, the normal bundle of a d'-branch, restricted to the deeper stratum, is the direct sum of the chosen normal line bundles. This is exactly where normal crossings is used.
- (199)–(200): Grothendieck then sets up the same data for chains d ≥ d' ≥ d'' over D(d,d',d''). Pulling back gives three lattices, three tori and (next page) three torsors. Further pullback via the three projections to D_{d,d'}, D_{d',d''} and D_{d,d''} gives the transitivity (cocycle) compatibilities (202). He calls these 'tautological'. The next page announces a non-tautological relation that uses the normal-crossings hypothesis.

Purpose. This is the combinatorial and torsor data of the 'structure at infinity' of M̄_{g,ν}. It is needed to describe fundamental groupoids at infinity: the π_1 of a punctured tubular neighbourhood of a stratum is an extension of π_1(stratum) by T_d(1), together with the specialisation and generisation maps between strata. In modern terms it is the stratified description of the log structure of a normal-crossings pair: characteristic sheaf M̄^gp = T_d on D_d, cospecialisation maps Z^d → Z^{d'}, and the G_m-torsors of the log structure, which are the deformation to the normal cone torsors.
